okay okay beat beat I'm just glad we're  still alive  sometimes video games confuse the hell  out of me how is it that a game is full  of diverse objectives and activities as  something like assasin creed black flag  could bore me after several honest  attempts to love it and conversely how  is it that a largely incomplete game in  a genre that hasn't seen mainstream  success in years could entranced me so  deeply ever since playing a very early  preview build of armikrog these are the  questions that have been plaguing my  thoughts what is it about this little  claymation based point-and-click  adventure that can make me feel  excitement and intrigue more  successfully than a multi-million dollar  project in a beloved series of games  well in spite of the placeholder visuals  temporary voice acting lack of music or  ambient audio in the incomplete nature  of the whole project armikrog is already  a game with heart what it lacks in  triple-a polish it makes up for in  spades with weird understated humor head  scratching puzzles environmental  atmosphere and a wealth of character as  I wandered the world of armikrog I felt  the alien mess of it all  nothing was labeled or explained I  wasn't sure where the world I was going  to explore began or ended nothing was  telling me where to go or what my role  was in all of this I simply was allowed  to feel out the world as I saw fit until  I began to piece together the earliest  elements of the first puzzles and this  hands-off approach is something that I  often longed for and more curated gaming  experiences to harken back to the  Assassin's Creed example sure the  opening of Black Flag gives you  background on who you are in the world  and it leaves you with context for your  adventure but imagine for a moment if  you were simply tossed into the deep end  with no texts no cutscenes no dialogue  the impression the world had on you  would be entirely different you wouldn't  be fed any preformed ideas about what  this place is or why you inhabit it as  it exists being fed so much expository  information right off the bat is like  opening the Hobbit to find a list of all  the characters with illustrations of  what they definitively look like you  would never have the chance to allow  your imagination to get creative with  the diegetic universe you're engaging  it's almost as if the measures taken to  ensure that the player gets the intended  experience is actually preventing the  player from finding their own unique  subjective experience which in my  opinion is much more important because  puzzles are such a central element to  any point-and-click adventure I must  also make note here of the strength of  the puzzles in this early section of the  game one involved entering a passcode of  sorts with no text to najin and  direction the solution spoiler alert  comes through a few sections of the game  where beak beak you're talking alien dog  companion must fend her off on his own  giving you access to a black and white  dog vision of sorts that can reveal  hidden clues and better yet I never even  found all three necessary clues after  finding the first two I tried to every  possible candidate for the third clue  until the door just popped open hell I  don't even know if there is a third clue  hidden somewhere and the fact that I may  never know excites me there's an element  of mystery in the absence of concrete  explanation that makes this feel less  like a linear tour of puzzles stitched  together with narrative and more like an  indifferent world filled with obstacles  through which I'm progressing absorption  at this level is incredibly important to  me in any game experience as soon as I  begin to lose that trance of novelty  that comes from being lost in the games  world my mind begins to decide the  mechanics of the gameplay to a degree  that I'm no longer entranced at this  point quest missions and objectives  simply become chores instead of fun  activities it doesn't matter if your  world is littered with nine thousand  procedurally generated side quests if I  don't care about my role in them or the  world that they impact armikrog may not  even be a complete game but I know for a  fact that I'll be playing it upon  release because it's a game of heart and  a real creative force behind it because  the game is incomplete and therefore so  reliant on these characters in this  world to carry it there's a  figure-ground shift in my typical  approach to games my relentlessly  analytical mind was no longer looking to  game the system or figure out the  mechanism behind the curtain as it so  frequently does with other games instead  I was simply immersed and invested and  that's why we love games in the first  place isn't it not because they  force-feed us excitement but because  they create a medium through which we  can find it for ourselves
 

